WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:07.420
In this lesson we will look at another kind of
product of vectors: the scalar triple

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:18.030
product. The scalar triple product of vectors
a, b and c is

3
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:24.760
the dot product of the cross product of a and
b with vector c. Property one:

4
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:31.420
if we swap the cross and dot product signs,
the value of the triple

5
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:38.260
product does not change. Property two: if we
multiply the vectors in cyclic order,

6
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:44.990
the triple product does not change. Property
three:

7
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:51.350
swapping any two vectors changes the sign of
the triple product.

8
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:58.270
Property four: if the triple product of three
vectors equals zero,

9
00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:04.239
then one of the factors is zero or two of the
vectors

10
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:09.420
are collinear; and if the vectors are nonzero,

11
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.640
they are coplanar.

12
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.240
The triple product can be set equal to the
determinant of a matrix whose rows

13
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.250
are the coordinates of the vectors. It looks
like this.

14
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.539
The first row holds the coordinates of the
first vector,

15
00:01:27.539 --> 00:01:30.300
then come the coordinates of the second
vector,

16
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.680
and the third row contains the coordinates of
the third vector.

17
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.437
Now let us look at an example of finding a
triple product.

18
00:01:40.437 --> 00:01:43.230
Three vectors are given: a with coordinates

19
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:49.820
three, four, five, b with coordinates four,
five and minus three,

20
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.830
and c with coordinates two, three, six.

21
00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:02.520
We need to find the triple product of vectors
a, b and c. To do this,

22
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.540
we form a third-order determinant.

23
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.780
We find the value of the determinant by the
triangle rule.

24
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.084
Following the rule, we compute the sum and
difference of

25
00:02:15.084 --> 00:02:19.080
the products of the entries. We do the
calculations.

26
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.340
So the triple product of the vectors equals
seven.

27
00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:33.720
What can we determine using the triple
product? First,

28
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:40.620
the mutual orientation of vectors a, b and c,
based on the following reasoning.

29
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:47.760
If the triple product is greater than zero,
the vectors form a right-handed triple,

30
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.290
and otherwise a left-handed triple.

31
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:58.750
The triple product lets us determine whether
vectors are coplanar.

32
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:05.720
Let us write the coplanarity condition: for
nonzero vectors a, b and c

33
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:13.400
to be coplanar, it is necessary and sufficient
that their triple product is zero.

34
00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.550
Let us look at an example. Three vectors are

35
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.990
given: a with coordinates one, one, zero, b

36
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:26.440
with coordinates one, one and minus one,

37
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:33.160
and c with coordinates one, one, two.

38
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.589
We need to determine whether these vectors are
coplanar.

39
00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:39.590
Let us form, from the coordinates of the
vectors,

40
00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:46.400
a third-order determinant. We find its value
by the triangle rule:

41
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.577
following the rule, we compute the sum and
difference

42
00:03:50.577 --> 00:03:53.020
of the products of the entries.

43
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.620
The calculation gives zero.

44
00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.600
So vectors a, b and c are coplanar.

45
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:10.821
The triple product lets us find the volume of
the parallelepiped built on these

46
00:04:10.821 --> 00:04:15.960
vectors: the volume equals the absolute value
of the triple product.

47
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:23.481
We can also find the volume of the triangular
pyramid built on these vectors:

48
00:04:23.481 --> 00:04:31.100
it equals one sixth of the absolute value of
the triple product of a, b and c.

49
00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.882
Let us look at an example of finding the
volume

50
00:04:35.882 --> 00:04:39.320
of a solid. Let the points A (0; 1; 0), B

51
00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:45.560
(3; −1; 5), C (1; 0; 3), D

52
00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:51.470
(−2; 3; 1) be given in a coordinate system.

53
00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.983
We need to find the volume of the triangular
pyramid

54
00:04:54.983 --> 00:04:57.820
with vertices at these points. To do this,

55
00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:04.340
we first find the coordinates of vectors AB,
AC and AD.

56
00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:13.010
Now we find the triple product: to do this, we
form a third-order determinant.

57
00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.811
We find the value of the determinant:

58
00:05:15.811 --> 00:05:19.520
it is minus seven. Then the volume of the
pyramid

59
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.000
equals seven sixths of a cubic unit.

60
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:32.580
So, in this lesson we studied another kind of
product of vectors: the scalar triple

61
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:38.830
product. It can be used to find the volumes of
solids

62
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:42.576
and to determine the orientation of vectors in
space.
